Subject: [PATCH 079/119] docs: the S3 debug cvar, the spec status line, and
 what will look wrong until S4

ConsoleReference gains r_SceneReferredDebug, in the "Live tuning" section next to
the two cvars it belongs with. Both modes get the reasoning and not just the
number: mode 1 is the A/B, and the point of it is that almost everything this
pipeline changes moves the picture, so toggling one thing at a time is the only way
to attribute a difference; mode 2 is the acceptance test, and an EMPTY frame is the
pass, because Rec.709's primaries lie inside AP1 and the forward matrix is
therefore entirely non-negative. Anything that lights up means the frame was
already broken upstream or the matrix is not being applied to what we think it is.
The entry also says where r_HDRDebug 3 sits relative to it - same test, end of the
chain instead of at the conversion point.

SceneReferredSpec.md S3 gets its status line, with the two amendments the stage
earned: the AP1 luminance weights arrive as a per-view CONSTANT stored as a delta
from Rec.709, not as the shader permutation section 5 implied (the DOF gather
evaluates them 121 times per pixel); and the third bullet's clamp sweep came back
EMPTY, because every colour-data clamp between the HDR target and the tone map is
already owned by S0's formats, S2's shafts, S4's display wall or S7's thresholds,
and our own effects clamp nothing but masks.

README: nothing the user sets changes, and it says so. The one thing it adds is a
paragraph on what to expect - the film curve and the sRGB encode are still at the
end of the chain and are now being fed ACEScg values while believing they are
Rec.709, so colours read a little desaturated and hue-shifted. That is the expected
middle of a two-part change and it is written down here so nobody spends an evening
chasing it. S4 replaces both with the ACES output transform, which is what the space
was widened for in the first place.

