+### r_SceneReferredDebug
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0 |
+| Purpose | Diagnostic for the **Rec.709 to ACEScg working-space conversion** (stage S3), the one 3x3 matrix that turns the frame into the space the whole post chain works in. Only has any effect while the scene-referred path is actually running. 0 = off. 1 = **skip** the conversion, leaving the frame in Rec.709 while everything else about the scene-referred pipeline stays on. 2 = **paint** any texel the conversion could not produce: red for NaN or infinity, green for a negative channel. |
+
+Mode 1 is the A/B. Almost everything this pipeline changes - the exposure, the light units, the pi
+convention - changes the picture, and so does the primaries move. Toggling this cvar is the only
+way to see the primaries change on its own, without turning the rest off.
+
+Mode 2 is the acceptance test, and it is worth understanding why an *empty* frame is the pass.
+Rec.709's primaries lie inside AP1, so the forward matrix is entirely non-negative: no
+non-negative Rec.709 colour can come out of it negative. Nothing should ever light up. If
+something does, it means the frame was already carrying a NaN or a negative before the conversion,
+or the matrix is not being applied to what we think it is - both real bugs, neither of them a
+property of the transform.
+
+The marked texels are painted at 64x, far above anything the tone map or the display transform
+resolves, so a mark is unmistakably a flat primary and cannot be confused with scene content.
+
+`r_HDRDebug 3` runs the same NaN / negative test at the **end** of the post chain instead, after
+depth of field, the glare family and motion blur have all had their turn. Two probes, two
+questions: mode 2 asks "did the matrix do something impossible", `r_HDRDebug 3` asks "did anything
+downstream".
+
